      "math_academy_id": "e-8441",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\cosh^2{x} + \\sinh^2{x}\\ \\, \\textrm{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\cos{2x}= \\cos^2{x}-\\sin^2{x}\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cosh 2x = \\cosh^2{x} + \\sinh^2{x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {cosh}^{2}⁡x+{sinh}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
